        'The EU AI Act is the European Union’s regulation governing how artificial intelligence is developed and used, and it is widely described as the first comprehensive legal framework for AI. It entered into force on 1 August 2024, with its obligations phasing in over several years; the bans on prohibited practices began applying in February 2025.',
        'The law is built around risk. A small set of uses deemed unacceptable, such as social scoring, are banned outright. High-risk systems, including those used in hiring, education, credit decisions and critical infrastructure, must meet requirements on risk management, data quality, documentation and human oversight. Lighter transparency duties apply to things like chatbots and AI-generated content, so people know when they are dealing with a machine. Providers of general-purpose models, such as large language models, face a separate set of obligations.',
